<p>Works over his 54 year career include: Base for Mandan's Theodore Roosevelt "Roughrider" statue Obelisk recognizing Chief Four Bears' role in the 1868 Fort Laramie Treaty Blue Thunder stone plaque over Collins Ave Civic Building (current Fire Hall) 1938 gravestone for Alexander McKenzie in St. Mary's Cemetery-Bismarck 1960 gravestone for Captain Grant Marsh in St. Mary's Cemetery-Bismarck Rybnicek and Mandan Mayor Clyde Byerly c. 1940</p>

<p>But he claimed the most pride for his work at the North Dakota Peace Garden, namely the Canadian-American Young Citizen's monument. Sponsored by the Young Citizens League of ND, the 1950 mahogany granite marker was paid for by donated pennies from the state's school children. He also attributed its inscription as being the most sincere of all "Under God Let Child Love Child and Strife Will Cease." Rybnicek also carved the stone marking the America Elm tree also donated by the Young Citizens League and placed on the State Capitol grounds.But history will most remember the craftsman for his work on North Dakota's contribution to the Washington Memorial in our nation's capital, and the final cornerstone in our state's Capitol.In 1933, Hynek returned to his practice of using native North Dakota fieldstone when he was recommended by Governor (and former Mandan resident) George Schafer to carve the cornerstone for the new State Capitol building. However this approach was for historical significance rather than cost. Rybnicek credited this work as his most difficult challenge as it had to be fabricated to fit an existing opening late in the project's construction.</p>

